    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the codebase. Let me provide you with a summary of what was done and confirm everything is working properly.\n\n## Problem Identified\nThe issue was in the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py`. The function was using Python's built-in `round()` function, which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even). This caused tax calculations like 12.5 cents to be rounded down to 12 instead of up to 13 as expected by the test cases.\n\n## Solution Implemented\nI modified the `round_half_up` function to explicitly implement \"round half up\" behavior instead of relying on Python's banker's rounding:\n\n**Key Change Made:**\n- Updated the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py` to use explicit rounding logic that always rounds .5 away from zero\n- This ensures 12.5 cents rounds to 13, and 51.5 cents rounds to 52, matching the expected test behavior\n\n## Verification Completed\n- \u2705 The specific failing test case now passes: 5% of $2.50 (250 cents) correctly rounds 12.5 cents up to 13 cents\n- \u2705 All existing tests continue to pass (85/85 tests passing)\n- \u2705 The fix handles all test cases in `test_regression_tax_rounding.py` correctly\n- \u2705 Both positive and negative number rounding works properly\n\nThe fix is minimal, targeted, and preserves all existing functionality while correcting the rounding behavior to match the expected test outcomes. The entire test suite now passes successfully.",
